Discussion
Given the critical role of CHK1 in G2 checkpoint regulation, especially in cancer types in which G1 regulation is impaired owing to TP53 loss, in the current study we investigated the role of CHK1 targeting in SCLC using a large number of molecularly characterized cell lines and animal models. We confirmed that CHK1 is overexpressed in SCLC patient tumors and studied the effect of targeted CHK1 knockdown and pharmacologic inhibition in vitro and in vivo. We found that a second-generation CHK1 inhibitor, LY2606368, had remarkable single-agent activity, enhanced the cytotoxic potential of clinically relevant dose of cisplatin (including in platinum-resistant models), and, when combined with th

